erately.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nResolution V designs are applicable in scenarios where researchers need to study multiple factors and their interactions without the risk of confounding main effects with lower-order interactions. They are particularly suitable for complex experiments where understanding two-factor interactions is critical, but the resources for a full factorial design are limited.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of data from a Resolution V design typically involves:\n1. Conducting an ANOVA to assess the significance of main effects and two-factor interactions.\n2. Estimating the effects using least squares estimation.\n3. Checking for model assumptions, including normality and homoscedasticity of residuals.\n4. Interpreting the results in the context of the experimental design.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \nThe primary advantages of Resolution V designs include:\n- The ability to study a larger number of factors without confounding critical effects.\n- Enhanced understanding of interactions among factors, particularly two-factor interactions.\n- Efficient use of resources, allowing for more complex experimental designs within limited sample sizes.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \nDespite their strengths, Resolution V designs have limitations:\n- They require larger sample sizes, which may not always be feasible.\n- The potential for confounding between two-factor and three-factor interactions can complicate interpretation.\n- The complexity of the design may lead to challenges in implementation and analysis.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRelated designs include Resolution IV and Resolution III fractional factorial designs, which offer different levels of resolution and confounding. Full factorial designs can also be considered when resources allow for the study of all possible combinations of factors.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing a R